I assume you are running this under Unix (a little more platform
information would have been a good help).  I also assume you didn't
install under your user ID.  There could be a couple of problems:

1) The installation went wrong and the file perm wasn't created.
2) The binary called nethack (not the shell script) wasn't set as a 'setuid'
   program.  This allows you to run nethack as the person who owns the
   playing area
3) The program IS setuid and the playing area isn't local to your machine,
   its mounted NFS, and the remote machine doesn't allow the machine you are
   running it from to write to the NFS disk, even with a setuid program.
   The way to test this:
   - Become the uid of the person who owns the playing area from your
     machine
   - rlogin into the machine where the playing area actually lives.  If
     the system asks you for a password, then what I'm saying is true.
   The way to solve this is to either:
   - Change the playing area's ownership to someone who can rlogin
     across machines without a password
   - Change the NFS setup to release restrictions across machines.
4) The program isn't setuid and you the protections on the playing area
   are improperly set

Note:  IF the problem was the setuid problem, and that's because the
installer didn't want to expose your system to a security breach to
install nethack as setuid root, then ask him/her to set it up as setuid
games.


